          "type": "turn",
          "question": "From what age does guidance describe screening for type 2 diabetes?",
          "answer": "Guidance on screening for type 2 diabetes generally recommends that all adults begin screening at age 35. Additionally, younger adults who are overweight or obese and have one or more additional risk factors should also be screened. These risk factors include having a first-degree relative with diabetes, belonging to a high-risk racial or ethnic group, having established cardiovascular disease, high blood pressure, low HDL cholesterol, elevated triglycerides, polycystic ovary syndrome, physical inactivity, or a history of gestational diabetes. \n\nThis information comes from the \"National diabetes association standards of care and preventive services guidance (educational summary)\" on screening for type 2 diabetes.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
